  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Package Managers -->
  <section id="package-managers">
    <h2>Package Managers</h2>
    <p>Tether syncs global packages across machines. Enable managers in config:</p>
    <Code code={`tether config set packages.brew.enabled true
tether config set packages.npm.enabled true`} />

    <h3>Homebrew</h3>
    <p>Syncs formulae, casks, and taps via Brewfile format. Casks include desktop apps like VS Code, Slack, Microsoft Office, and most macOS applications installed via <code>brew install --cask</code>. Tether skips casks on Linux.</p>
    <Code code={`# Manifest: manifests/Brewfile
brew "ripgrep"
brew "fd"
cask "visual-studio-code"
cask "slack"
cask "1password"`} />
    <p>The Brewfile has no versions, because Homebrew installs only the current release. Taps outside <code>homebrew/*</code> must be in <code>packages.brew.trusted_taps</code>. See <a href="#package-security">Package Security</a>.</p>

    <h3>npm</h3>
    <p>Syncs global npm packages. Each line records the version that a machine installed.</p>
    <Code code={`# Manifest: manifests/npm.txt
typescript@5.6.3
eslint@9.14.0
prettier`} />
    <p>A line without a version still works. Tether then installs the newest release that passes the release-age limit.</p>

    <h3>pnpm / bun / gem</h3>
    <p>One package per line. pnpm and bun use <code>name@1.2.3</code>, as npm does. gem uses <code>name:1.2.3</code>.</p>

    <h3>uv (Python)</h3>
    <p>Syncs global Python tools installed via <a href="https://github.com/astral-sh/uv" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-primary hover:underline">uv</a>.</p>
    <Code code={`# Enable uv sync
tether config set packages.uv.enabled true

# Manifest: manifests/uv.txt
ruff==0.7.2
black
mypy`} />

    <h3>Versions</h3>
    <p>A version must be a concrete release: semver for npm, pnpm and bun, PEP 440 for uv, and a RubyGems version for gem. Tether skips a line with a dist-tag such as <code>latest</code>, or with a range, and shows a warning.</p>
    <p>When machines have different versions, the manifest keeps the newest one by the rules of each ecosystem. A sync installs the newest version that a trusted machine record lists.</p>
    <p>A sync merges into the manifests. It adds or updates lines only from this machine's record and the records it trusts. It keeps the lines of other machines. It removes a line only when no machine record in the repo lists that package. A pin moves only to a newer version, or off a version that no record lists.</p>
    <p>A package that fails to install waits 24 hours before Tether tries it again, or until its version changes. <code>tether packages --list</code> and the dashboard Packages tab show failed installs.</p>
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Package Security -->
  <section id="package-security">
    <h2>Package Security</h2>
    <p>Anyone who can push to your sync repo can edit a manifest. Tether checks each synced package before a package manager runs. A package that fails a check waits in the <a href="#approval-inbox">approval inbox</a>. For the threats and limits, see <a href="/supply-chain">Supply-chain security</a>.</p>
    <FlowDiagram label="BEFORE A SYNCED PACKAGE INSTALLS" caption="Tether runs these checks again on every sync, for new and held packages." steps={[
      { icon: 'key', title: 'Trusted record', detail: 'A trusted machine lists this version' },
      { icon: 'shield', title: 'Policy checks', detail: 'Name · Tap · Release age · OSV' },
      { icon: 'box', title: 'Install or hold', detail: 'Hold it in the inbox if a check fails' },
    ]} />

    <h3>Checks by package manager</h3>
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface">
          <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4">Check</th>
          <th class="text-left py-2 pr-4">Managers</th>
          <th class="text-left py-2">Result</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Trusted machine record</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">All</td>
          <td class="py-2">Holds a package that no trusted, signed record lists at that version</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Name validation</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">All</td>
          <td class="py-2">Skips names that look like flags, URLs, paths, tarballs, or <code>git+</code>, <code>github:</code>, <code>file:</code> and <code>link:</code> specs</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Release age (default 7 days)</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">npm 11.10+, pnpm 10.16+, bun 1.3+, uv</td>
          <td class="py-2">Skips releases newer than the limit. Holds packages for gem and older npm, pnpm and bun, which cannot enforce it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Install scripts off</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">npm, pnpm, bun</td>
          <td class="py-2">Installs and upgrades with scripts off, except for packages in <code>packages.allow_scripts</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">OSV malware check</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">npm, pnpm, bun, uv, gem</td>
          <td class="py-2">Blocks a release with a <code>MAL-</code> advisory. Shows other advisories as warnings</td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-surface/50">
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Trusted taps</td>
          <td class="py-2 pr-4">Homebrew</td>
          <td class="py-2">Holds taps outside <code>homebrew/*</code> and <code>packages.brew.trusted_taps</code>, and their formulae and casks</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Homebrew has no release-age limit and no OSV data. Tether does not check brew packages against OSV.</p>

    <h3>OSV checks</h3>
    <p>Tether finds the release that would install: the newest stable release older than the release-age limit. It checks that release against OSV and installs it pinned. Thus the release that OSV checked is the release that installs.</p>
    <p>Sometimes Tether cannot find that release, for example from a registry that needs a login. OSV then checks all releases, and only <code>MAL-</code> advisories count. The package waits as "malicious releases, install version unknown". You can approve it after a warning.</p>
    <p>A network failure does not block a sync. The dashboard and <code>tether packages approve</code> ask before they install without an OSV result. Tether uses <code>curl</code> with a 10-second limit.</p>

    <h3>Install scripts</h3>
    <p>Add a package to <code>packages.allow_scripts</code> to run its own install scripts:</p>
    <Code code={`tether config set packages.allow_scripts '["esbuild"]'`} />
    <p>pnpm 10.4 and later, and npm 12 and later, run scripts for that package only. Older npm and pnpm would also run the scripts of its dependencies. On those versions, its scripts stay off, and Tether shows a warning once.</p>

    <h3>Homebrew taps</h3>
    <Code code={`tether config set packages.brew.trusted_taps '["oven-sh/bun"]'`} />
    <p>Tether finds the tap of a short name, such as <code>bun</code>, before it installs it. An approved formula installs by its qualified name, such as <code>homebrew/core/wget</code>. Upgrades include only outdated packages from trusted taps. Tether updates trusted taps with <code>git pull --ff-only</code> and does not run <code>brew update</code>.</p>

    <h3>Other safeguards</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Every package manager runs in the empty directory <code>~/.tether/run</code>, not in your current directory</li>
      <li>Tether rejects names and versions that a manager reads as a local file, such as <code>.tgz</code>, <code>.gem</code>, <code>.whl</code> or a Homebrew <code>.rb</code> file</li>
      <li><code>gem install</code> and <code>gem update</code> use <code>--remote</code></li>
      <li>The daemon does not auto-upgrade a manager that cannot enforce the release-age limit</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="approval-inbox">Approval inbox</h3>
    <p>Held packages, taps, and machine keys wait in <code>~/.tether/inbox.json</code>. The inbox stays on this machine and never syncs.</p>
    <Code code={`tether packages inbox
tether packages approve npm:typescript 5.6.3
tether packages reject npm:typescript 5.6.3`} />
    <ul>
      <li>An approval covers that version only. For Homebrew, it covers only the tap that the name resolved to</li>
      <li>A rejected version, tap, or key is not offered again</li>
      <li>A release with a <code>MAL-</code> advisory cannot be approved</li>
      <li>Approving a package from an untrusted tap does not trust the tap</li>
      <li>Tether refuses a decision when the item changed after you saw it, and shows the item again</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In a terminal, <code>tether sync</code> asks about held items. You can review each one, install all, or decide later. "Install all" leaves machine keys and packages with a failed signature for their own decision. On the dashboard, use the Security tab.</p>
    <p>To hold packages from trusted machines too, set <code>packages.auto_install_from_trusted = false</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Machine Trust -->
  <section id="machine-trust">
    <h2>Machine Trust</h2>
    <p>Each machine has an SSH signing key in <code>~/.tether/signing_key</code>. Tether makes it on <code>tether init</code>, or on the first sync after an upgrade. Each sync signs this machine's record, <code>machines/&lt;id&gt;.json</code>, which lists its packages and versions.</p>
    <p>Each machine keeps its own trust list in <code>~/.tether/trusted_keys</code>. The list never syncs. It starts with this machine's own key.</p>

    <h3>Trust a machine</h3>
    <p>A new machine key waits in the inbox as "trust machine". Before you trust it, compare its fingerprint. On the new machine, find the row marked "(this machine)":</p>
    <Code code="tether machines list" />
    <p>On each of your other machines, trust it with that fingerprint:</p>
    <Code code={`tether machines trust work-laptop SHA256:...
# or
tether packages approve machine:work-laptop SHA256:...`} />
    <p>Tether trusts the key only if the fingerprint matches. Without a fingerprint, it shows the current one and asks, in a terminal only. To stop trusting a machine:</p>
    <Code code="tether machines untrust work-laptop" />

    <h3>What trust means</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>A package installs on its own only when a trusted, signed record lists that exact version</li>
      <li>Trust is transitive. When you trust a machine, you also trust the packages it installed, whoever installed them. A package you rejected on this machine stays blocked</li>
      <li>Manifests and commit signatures do not grant trust. Commit signatures are an audit trail only</li>
      <li>A sync adds manifest lines only from this machine's record and the records it trusts. A line in a manifest grants no trust</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Protection against changed records</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Changed key</strong> — When a trusted machine signs with a different key, Tether warns you. It does not trust that record until you approve the new key</li>
      <li><strong>Failed signature</strong> — Tether ignores a record that fails its signature and warns: "Record for &lt;id&gt; fails its signature"</li>
      <li><strong>Replay</strong> — Each record has a generation that increases with every save. An older signed record from the same key, such as one from git history, does not count</li>
      <li><strong>Renamed machines</strong> — Trust never moves to another machine id on its own. A renamed machine waits in the inbox as a new machine</li>
      <li><strong>Your own record</strong> — This machine builds its record from <code>~/.tether/machine.json</code>, not from the repo copy</li>
    </ul>
    <p><code>tether machines list</code> and the dashboard Machines cards show each record as a sync reads it. A record that a sync ignores shows "replayed (ignored)" or "signature failed (ignored)".</p>

    <h3 id="old-machine-ids">Old machine ids</h3>
    <p>Builds before random machine ids used the hostname as the id. Tether marks a record that may be an old id of this machine. This is only a guess. Check that no other machine uses that hostname before you remove it.</p>
    <p><code>tether machines list</code> and <code>tether status</code> show the command that removes it. On the dashboard Machines tab, press <code>D</code>, then <code>y</code>.</p>
    <Code code="tether machines remove <old-id>" />
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Encryption -->
  <section id="encryption">
    <h2>Encryption & Security</h2>

    <h3>How Encryption Works</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>On first init, a random 256-bit key is generated</li>
      <li>Key is encrypted with your passphrase using <code>age</code></li>
      <li>Encrypted key is stored in the Git repo (safe to share)</li>
      <li>Dotfiles are encrypted with AES-256-GCM before commit</li>
      <li>On other machines, enter passphrase to decrypt the key</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Secret Detection</h3>
    <p>Before syncing, Tether scans for common secret patterns:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>AWS credentials (<code>AKIA...</code>)</li>
      <li>GitHub tokens (<code>ghp_...</code>, <code>gho_...</code>)</li>
      <li>Generic patterns (<code>API_KEY=</code>, <code>password=</code>)</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Add ignore patterns for false positives:</p>
    <Code code="tether ignore add 'EXAMPLE_KEY='" />

    <h3>Unlocking</h3>
    <p>If the daemon can't decrypt (key not cached), run:</p>
    <Code code="tether unlock" />
    <p>To clear the cached key:</p>
    <Code code="tether lock" />
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Team Sync -->
  <section id="team-sync">
    <h2>Team Sync</h2>
    <p>Share configurations and encrypted secrets across a team.</p>

    <h3>Setup</h3>
    <Code code={`# Interactive setup wizard
tether team setup

# Or add manually
tether team add git@github.com:org/team-dotfiles.git`} />
    <p>This clones the team repo and creates symlinks to team config files.</p>

    <h3>How It Works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Team configs are symlinked to their target locations</li>
      <li>If you have write access, your changes sync back</li>
      <li>Read-only access means you receive updates only</li>
      <li>Auto-inject adds <code>source</code> lines to your personal dotfiles (optional)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Team Secrets</h3>
    <p>Share encrypted secrets using <a href="https://age-encryption.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-primary hover:underline">age</a> public-key cryptography:</p>
    <Code code={`# Add your public key as a recipient
tether team secrets add-recipient age1...

# Set a secret (encrypted for all recipients)
tether team secrets set API_KEY

# Get a secret
tether team secrets get API_KEY`} />

    <h3>Organization Mapping</h3>
    <p>Map GitHub organizations to teams for automatic project secret sharing:</p>
    <Code code={`# Map org to team
tether team orgs add github.com/acme-corp

# Project secrets from that org are now shared with the team
tether team projects list`} />

    <h3>Project Secrets</h3>
    <p>Share .env files and project configs across team members:</p>
    <Code code={`# Share a project secret
tether team projects add .env

# Auto-migrate secrets from mapped orgs
tether team projects migrate

# Remove personal copies (use team version)
tether team projects purge-personal`} />

    <h3>Managing Teams</h3>
    <Code code={`tether team list          # Show all teams
tether team switch other   # Switch active team
tether team disable        # Disable without removing
tether team remove         # Remove team completely`} />
  </section>

  <hr class="my-12 border-surface" />

  <!-- Troubleshooting -->
  <section id="troubleshooting">
    <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>

    <h3>Daemon not running</h3>
    <Code code={`tether daemon start
tether daemon logs`} />

    <h3>Sync conflicts</h3>
    <Code code="tether resolve" />
    <p>For files an app rewrites on every machine, such as timestamps or caches, set a rule. Tether then settles conflicts without asking:</p>
    <Code code={`[[profiles.dev.dotfiles]]
path = ".claude/plugins/known_marketplaces.json"
on_conflict = "local"   # or "remote"; default "prompt"`} />
    <p>Or force one direction:</p>
    <Code code="tether sync --force" />

    <h3>Encryption issues</h3>
    <Code code={`# Unlock with passphrase
tether unlock

# Check if key exists
ls ~/.tether/sync/encryption.key.age`} />

    <h3>A package from another machine does not install</h3>
    <p>The package probably waits in the approval inbox. The inbox shows the reason for each item.</p>
    <Code code={`tether packages inbox
tether machines list   # Is the source machine trusted?`} />
    <p>If the source machine is not trusted, see <a href="#machine-trust">Machine Trust</a>. A package that failed to install waits 24 hours before Tether tries it again.</p>

    <h3>"Record for &lt;id&gt; fails its signature"</h3>
    <p>Someone edited that machine's record in the repo, or deleted its <code>.sig</code> file. Tether ignores the record until that machine signs a valid one. Run <code>tether sync</code> on that machine, then check <code>tether machines list</code>.</p>

    <h3>"Machine key changed" in the inbox</h3>
    <p>A trusted machine signed its record with a different key. This is expected after you reinstall Tether on that machine. Compare the fingerprint with <code>tether machines list</code> on that machine before you approve it.</p>

    <h3>"Secret detected" blocking sync</h3>
    <p>Either remove the secret or add an ignore pattern:</p>
    <Code code="tether ignore add 'MY_SAFE_PATTERN='" />

    <h3>Reset everything</h3>
    <Code code={`tether daemon stop
rm -rf ~/.tether
tether init`} />
    <p>This deletes the machine's signing key and trust list. Tether makes a new key, so your other machines see a changed key and must approve it. This machine must trust your other machines again.</p>

    <h3>View full logs</h3>
    <Code code="cat ~/.tether/daemon.log" />
  </section>
